      {
        "id": "W14.CH03",
        "title": "3. The living racer: common anatomical language",
        "intro": "The host remains an animal whose working anatomy has been deliberately rebuilt. The following terms define what the builder is manipulating; they are not additional mandatory equipment slots.",
        "entries": [
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.HOST",
            "title": "Host",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The persistent living roach that supplies the native foundation and receives grafts. Its identity continues through changes in equipment, finish, ownership and race career.",
              "Distinction": "A build is a configuration of this animal; a saved build is not another animal."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.BODYPLAN",
            "title": "Body plan",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The native distribution of length, width, mass, articulation and root-bearing surfaces. Lowline, Needleback and Broadback are established working examples, not an exhaustive species list.",
              "Distinction": "A narrow animal does not shrink bought limbs. Body-plan inheritance is a biology question, not a reason to copy the three-plan prototype globally."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.SEAM",
            "title": "Molt seam",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The proposed name for the rad roach’s mutable junction tissue. It incorporates donor anatomy and forms continuity with the host. It is an expression of the author’s assimilation premise.",
              "Distinction": "The name does not require ordinary adult insects to molt, imply general limb regrowth, or restrict all grafting to a juvenile window."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.ROOT",
            "title": "Graft root",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The interface through which a sect attaches and becomes supported, supplied and controlled. A root includes the tissues needed by its own function; different preparations expose different shapes.",
              "Distinction": "The root is not an arbitrary colored socket that rejects another insect family."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.ANCHOR",
            "title": "Load anchor",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The portion of a construction that passes force from the working sect into adequate supporting anatomy. A horn root, wing hinge and leg root impose different directions of load.",
              "Distinction": "A surface attachment that looks connected does not establish a useful load path."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.FASCICLE",
            "title": "Drive bundle",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "Contractile tissue plus the interfaces needed to apply its work. Mechanical output, control and supply must all reach the body part being driven.",
              "Distinction": "Visible size, buzzing and species reputation do not individually establish useful output."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.LINK",
            "title": "Gait linkage",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "Levers and coordinating junctions that define stroke travel, working force and timing. The native roach already has such structures.",
              "Distinction": "The part that changes travel is not necessarily the part that synchronizes release."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.CONTACT",
            "title": "Contact surface",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The actual anatomy touching a floor, wall, opponent or support. Material, area, orientation, loading and release all belong to its purpose.",
              "Distinction": "Foot traction, rail sliding, gripping jaws and braking material are not a single universal grip score."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.SUPPLY",
            "title": "Supply pathway",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The host and graft interfaces through which stored energy and necessary physiological support reach working tissues. The definition is functional; it does not assert a particular real-insect circulatory mechanism.",
              "Distinction": "Separate organs cannot each spend the entire same reserve independently."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.RETURN",
            "title": "Return path",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The route by which a displaced limb, plate or moving mass returns to its useful position. It includes elastic, muscular or damped elements where those exist.",
              "Distinction": "A spring can return energy; it does not automatically remove rebound or aim the returning limb."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.COMMAND",
            "title": "Motor and sensory junction",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "The connection between host control, local sensory information and a working graft. The shared host remains the actor; an installed arm does not bring an independent donor personality.",
              "Distinction": "A sensor supplies information, not strength or omniscient knowledge of the course."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.COLLAR",
            "title": "Prepared exchange collar",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Definition": "A deliberately maintained release boundary around a graft root that supports reversible workshop rebuilding. It trades preparation and care for predictable swaps.",
              "Distinction": "The collar helps reuse; it does not make assimilation possible in the first place, is not proprietary species permission, and does not guarantee every raw extraction is harmless."
            },
            "refs": [],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.JOINS",
            "title": "A functional installation has five kinds of continuity",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Attachment": "The graft remains seated through its required movements.",
              "Support": "Forces pass into the host rather than through a decorative or unsupported connection.",
              "Supply": "The working anatomy receives the resources it requires and has unobstructed necessary exchange surfaces.",
              "Control": "The host can activate, relax and interpret the graft to the extent required by the construction.",
              "Clearance": "The working and returning anatomy has physical room to move.",
              "Example": "A second drive bundle is meaningful when its supply and output can join a supported transmission. Simply attaching another muscle does not duplicate the entire leg system; conversely, a one-drive trial interface is not a universal law against multi-drive racers.",
              "Common experience": "A coordinated beginner kit supplies an understandable arrangement that solves these requirements. Experts can redesign them. Every novice is not required to perform five repetitive diagnostic rituals before a first race."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.ANATOMY.ROOT",
              "W14.ANATOMY.SUPPLY",
              "W14.MAP.DRIVE"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.REGIONS",
            "title": "The body has regions, not an immutable equipment grid",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Brow and head": "Good visibility and forward contact opportunities; horn or jaw load still needs a supported root. Split vanes may coexist around a central horn.",
              "Thorax": "Working muscle, major load transfer and limb roots compete through actual anatomy. This is where bracing and controlled flexibility matter most.",
              "Flanks": "Sensory stalks, articulated braces and rail guides need their own exposure, travel and support. Their working width may differ from folded width.",
              "Belly": "Reservoir position, compliant landings, suspended masses and contact brakes interact through clearance and distribution.",
              "Abdomen": "Storage and specialized glands affect distribution, supply and clearance. A rear weapon’s contents are not also available as the drive’s full fuel reserve.",
              "Dorsal envelope": "Protective plates, vanes and powered wings need different opening and working spaces. Split armor can coexist with a flight root where its actual arrangement allows it."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.ANATOMY.JOINS"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": []
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.ANATOMY.RACE_READINGS",
            "title": "What the public performance readings mean",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Speed and acceleration": "Pace and how quickly it is reached are outcomes of the working construction. They must remain desirable, intelligible comparisons; neither is a duplicate gene added on top of every contributing part.",
              "Grip and cornering": "Grip describes contact behavior under specified conditions. Cornering describes the whole racer negotiating a route while maintaining useful motion. These values can correlate without being interchangeable.",
              "Balance": "The construction’s ability to maintain or restore usable posture through sensing, support and action. It is not a universal reduction to all forms of damage.",
              "Hold-down": "An aerodynamic load tied to motion and surface arrangement. Its authored RRR usefulness must remain tied to air interaction, not a standing-still buff.",
              "Mass and reserve": "Actual carried material and available energy. Fill, hardware-like fixtures, living tissue and protection have distinguishable contributions.",
              "Observation discipline": "A comparison names its load, surface, configuration and relevant condition. A measured lap is a result, not proof that one selected gene caused every improvement."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.S01",
              "W14.MAP.SETS"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": []
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "W14.CH04",
        "title": "4. Sect reference catalogue: anatomy with a reason to exist",
        "intro": "Each entry states its physical subject, useful selection, fitting contribution, visible behavior and commercial use. Familiar names are retained. Recovered earlier concepts are identified as reference designs rather than falsely counted as new implemented products. A product’s complete anatomical allocation governs whether two preparations can come from one donor; the same tissue cannot be sold twice.",
        "entries": [
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.REDLINE",
            "title": "Cicada Redline",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "DRIVE",
              "Donor and source": "Furnace cicada; rooted pipe galleries, the Rooted signal pole and Root furnace. Adults supply working tissue; continued production needs the root-fed juvenile stage, not only an adult display cage.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "A ribbed thoracic muscle bundle with supported ends and preserved supply/control interfaces. Its visible pulse becomes purposeful limb or wing-drive work after integration.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Compare cadence under a declared load, useful force and conversion efficiency. Retain an efficient ordinary-cadence founder to complement a fast but hungry line. Noise and agitated movement are screening clues, not measurements.",
              "Builder contribution": "Route work to a suitable linkage and choose leverage independently. Supply must support the desired duty, with space for the bundle to work without pinching surrounding anatomy.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "Faster repeated working strokes when the rest of the build can use them. Foot-release bottlenecks or insufficient supply can prevent that output becoming pace.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Useful in sprint kits and flight-drive preparations. Ordinary output supports club upgrades; selected economical tissue serves longer heats. A complete root nursery supports a recurring supplier.",
              "Boundary": "It does not set leg reach or manufacture energy. The fictional Redline is not a claim that a real cicada’s sound-producing anatomy is a ready-made racing motor."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.DRIVE"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "redline",
              "W02.DONOR.CICADA"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.TORQUE",
            "title": "Scarab Torque Bundle",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "DRIVE",
              "Donor and source": "Kilnback scarab; protected furnace compost and Cooling compost. Retain healthy adults and complete grub/pupal development for repeat production.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Dense muscle columns with broad working attachments. The prepared block needs supporting anatomy capable of receiving its repeated load.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select useful force per material mass and sustained force under the intended operating demand. Large shell size is not sufficient. Frame traits from the same line are a separate product objective.",
              "Builder contribution": "Place the bundle near a practical output path and choose the gait ratio. A loaded body may need structural reinforcement; an empty decorative plate does not supply it.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "Forceful starts, climbing work and recovery after traffic when contact can transmit the force. Higher-force tissue need not have the highest unloaded cadence.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Loaded Open constructions, rough-course kits and drive commissions. Disjoint internal support plates can support a separate Boxbrace preparation where the harvest plan preserves them.",
              "Boundary": "A forceful drive does not automatically make a charge effective: approach, contact and horn-root support remain necessary."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.DRIVE"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "torque",
              "W02.DONOR.SCARAB"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.LOWSTEP",
            "title": "Mole-Cricket Lowstep",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "GAIT",
              "Donor and source": "Ballast mole cricket; Ballast trench, Ballast Banks and suitable loose root beds. Hip structure, toes and sleeve tissue have different allocation purposes.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Short hip levers and their working junctions, prepared to exchange stroke distance for force at the driven limbs.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select the mechanical-advantage range required by the intended construction and consistent joint geometry. Do not select maximum ratio as a universal optimum. A modest ratio with good articulation may fit the product better.",
              "Builder contribution": "Choose the working ratio through preparation and fine alignment. Match travel with the installed muscle and running spars rather than adding a second stride bonus.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "Shorter forceful strokes, useful starts and load handling. Extended clean straights may expose its limited travel per drive cycle.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Loaded kit production; hip assemblies sold separately from cleats. A breeder can supply a repeated geometry range rather than an alleged top-tier gene.",
              "Boundary": "Ratio is an operating choice. It does not create more work than the input supplies."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.GAIT"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "lowstep",
              "W12.REPAIR.01"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.LONGTHROW",
            "title": "Tiger-Beetle Longthrow",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "GAIT",
              "Donor and source": "Trackrunner tiger beetle; hardpan between sleepers and sunlit slab edges. This donor and assembly are retained from the earlier compendium, beyond the limited playable source roster.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Long, light hip-and-limb lever structures with separately identified distal contact tips.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select reach without unnecessary moving mass, and working articulation that preserves the intended stroke. Very long but heavy extremities can be a poor high-cadence combination.",
              "Builder contribution": "Match drive force, stroke travel and stance. A complete limb assembly includes its geometry; adding a separate limb-reach term must not count that length twice.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "Greater travel per useful stroke on suitable ground, with different launch and load behavior from Lowstep.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Sprint builders and replacement-limb makers seek selected reach bands. Non-premium individuals can still provide useful tips or another size of assembly.",
              "Boundary": "This is not a promise that long legs always win or that a juvenile automatically grows into a higher product tier."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.GAIT"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "Tiger-Beetle Longthrow",
              "W02.DONOR.TIGER"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.LOCKSTEP",
            "title": "Planthopper Lockstep",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "GAIT",
              "Donor and source": "Clockleaf planthopper; protected seepage reeds. Late juveniles are the working source in this fictional profile; adult founders provide the next cohorts. Retained earlier compendium concept.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "A paired coordinating structure that constrains the relative timing of linked hind actions. It is prepared with an intact coupling interface, not merely two decorative teeth.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select consistent phase alignment, retention of that alignment through use, and a manageable useful-stage window. Adult body size is not evidence that the juvenile coupling will be best.",
              "Builder contribution": "Align the paired output with the actual launch assembly. Keep leverage selection separate. Avoid overconstraining a gait that needs independently placed feet.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "More closely coordinated release can reduce unwanted rotation during an intended paired launch.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Synchronizer specialists can sell stage-matched components and separately retain adult founders. Scheduling and dependable preparation are valuable even without extreme peak output.",
              "Boundary": "Timing precision is not a second motor, a variable-ratio gearbox or a free increase to all running cadence."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.GAIT"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "Planthopper Lockstep",
              "W02.DONOR.CLOCK"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.STICKTOE",
            "title": "Sticktoe Contact Set",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "CONTACT",
              "Donor and source": "Glassfoot stick insect; Dewglass wall and Glazed Overgrowth. Preserve clean, intact contact tissue and enough suitable adults to continue the line.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Flexible foot-contact pads prepared in identified pairs. The contact material and its orientation are separate from the leg that carries it.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select purchase under load, release time and retention of useful contact after fouling. A fast-release ordinary-grip animal may be the missing founder for a highly adhesive line.",
              "Builder contribution": "Choose front, middle and rear allocations, contact area and orientation. Different pair preparations can serve one racer; matching colors are not required for mechanical compatibility.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "A planted foot holds; the unloaded foot peels away and cycles. Slow release visibly interrupts a fast gait even when static grip looks impressive.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Technical and fast-contact kits; replacement pairs; alternatively prepared Gripstrips. A whole brood can serve different contact-area and performance specifications.",
              "Boundary": "Running pads and Gripstrips cannot both consume the same pad source. Better grip and faster release can coexist as a genuine improvement."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.CONTACT"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "sticktoe",
              "W02.DONOR.STICK"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.CLEATS",
            "title": "Mole-Cricket Cleats",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "CONTACT",
              "Donor and source": "Ballast mole cricket; loose ballast and root-bank habitat. Toe damage matters to immediate use without rewriting hereditary potential.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Toothed contact ends that obtain purchase through the surface rather than relying only on smooth adhesive contact.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select useful tooth geometry, release and resistance to clogging on the declared substrate. Different particle sizes can favor different preparations.",
              "Builder contribution": "Choose toe exposure and pair placement. Keep hip leverage independent when the donor’s other regions supply a Lowstep assembly.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "Less slip on suitable loose material; a different contact and release pattern on smooth metal.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Rubble-run kits, local ash-course stock and field-supply orders. Different toes and hip structures can become disjoint products from one appropriately preserved donor.",
              "Boundary": "Loose-ground performance is a surface relationship, not a permanent all-surface traction bonus."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.CONTACT"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "cleats"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.BOXBRACE",
            "title": "Scarab Boxbrace",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "FRAME",
              "Donor and source": "Kilnback scarab; Cooling compost and furnace beds. The source is internal support anatomy, not every visible outer plate.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Interlocking ribs arranged between the working roots they must support. Open spaces accommodate living tissue and articulated joints.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select load support per mass, useful deformation and dimensional consistency. A light strong brace can be a real improvement, not a compulsory trade for some unrelated weakness.",
              "Builder contribution": "Orient the ribs along the intended load path. Protect articulation instead of binding every joint rigidly. A horn support and a wing-root support need not be the same pattern.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "The working root moves against support rather than collapsing its surrounding body. Its benefit depends on where force is actually applied.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Structural kits and custom reinforcement work. Breeders supply plates; skilled fitters arrange them for particular hosts and working organs.",
              "Boundary": "It is not general armor. A rigid shell can transmit a harsh landing; suspension still has its own job."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.FRAME"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "boxbrace"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.FLEXRAIL",
            "title": "Roach Flexrail",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "FRAME",
              "Donor and source": "Rad-roach supporting plates from separately identified donor or retained native material. A starter host’s own structure is not a free duplicate product.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Overlapping small ribs linked by flexible seams, preserving motion while providing an organized supporting path.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select repeatable flex and adequate support for the intended working load. Uncontrolled softness and helpful compliance are different properties.",
              "Builder contribution": "Set overlap and the joints allowed to flex. A broad soft cradle can be unsuitable for a concentrated horn-root load without additional local support.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "Controlled body compliance across uneven sections, without demanding that every impact be absorbed in the legs alone.",
              "Hobby and trade": "General club preparations, restorations and light custom projects. A native foundation may already provide enough support and require no replacement.",
              "Boundary": "This recovered reference design is not an extra global mandatory frame swap or a claim that a living host can donate its own essential structure twice."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.FRAME"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "Roach Flexrail"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.GYROPODS",
            "title": "Fly Gyropods",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "BALANCE",
              "Donor and source": "Sumpfly; damp service voids, Seep ledges and the Rooted signal pole. Protect both sensing stalks.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Paired moving sensory stalks and a prepared signal junction. The anatomy remains distinct from the feet or braces that execute a correction.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select useful rotational sensitivity, signal delay and pair calibration. Useful information under motion matters more than stalk size.",
              "Builder contribution": "Preserve exposure, paired orientation and connection to an available response. Fitting under an obstructive shell can defeat the selected sensitivity.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "An earlier or less biased correction can begin through the host’s existing anatomy. The correction itself still needs ground contact or an appropriate airborne actuator.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Fast-contact kits, Hopper builders and Flyer instrumentation. Prepared matched pairs are a useful product; unmatched single fragments are not automatically equivalent.",
              "Boundary": "No passive armor, extra traction force or supernatural awareness of hidden rivals. The real reference describes halteres as rotation sensors, not stabilizing ballast."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.BALANCE",
              "W14.S13"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "gyropods"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.COUNTERBRACES",
            "title": "Mantis Counterbraces",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "BALANCE",
              "Donor and source": "Saltglass mantis; Hanging frames and prey-facing Saltglass perches. The same forelimb source can instead be prepared as Catchclaws.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Articulated forelimbs configured to open into a support position; the support-only preparation has no operative grasping attack.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select useful extension, firm support and responsive folding. Giant span is not the sole goal: the brace must reach a surface in the intended posture.",
              "Builder contribution": "Set its working support position and folded clearance. Distinguish a brace that is genuinely converted from a weapon merely renamed on a label.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "Adds a real supporting contact when deployed. It may broaden the racer’s passing envelope during use.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Speedsters-compatible support conversions where their actual capability qualifies, as well as Open stability sets and fitting commissions.",
              "Boundary": "The brace does nothing to support the animal if it cannot reach a suitable surface. The donor cannot supply a second untouched forelimb pair."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.BALANCE"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "counterbraces"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.GUARDKNUCKLES",
            "title": "Beetle Guardknuckles",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "BALANCE",
              "Donor and source": "Gutterback darkling side-shell stock, allocated instead of complete Railshoes where regions overlap. This elaborates an earlier reference concept.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Rounded high contact stops on short supported mounts. Their height is selected to catch developing lean against a guide wall.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select shell integrity and support at the needed geometry. Surface smoothness is relevant to sliding but not the complete anti-lean purpose.",
              "Builder contribution": "Position relative to normal body posture and wall height. Keep them clear during ordinary running if their job is only to catch a lean.",
              "Visible racing consequence": "A visible contact limits further tipping when the appropriate wall exists.",
              "Hobby and trade": "Narrow-channel racing, beginner handling packs and replacement stops. The same stock family can serve anti-lean products or sliding-guide products.",
              "Boundary": "This is the direct contact-style stabilizer analogue. It is neither a sensory organ nor a powered corrective limb."
            },
            "refs": [
              "W14.MAP.BALANCE",
              "W14.S09"
            ],
            "legacy_refs": [
              "Beetle Guardknuckles"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "W14.PART.SPRINGKNEES",
            "title": "Locust Springknees",
            "status": "working_compendium_proposal",
            "fields": {
              "Reference role": "LANDING",
              "Donor and source": "Switchyard hopper hind joints; the historic product name is a workshop pattern, not a demand that a different donor be invented.",
              "Prepared anatomy": "Compliant joint structures prepared for landing on retained running limbs. They do not include every structure needed for an active launch.",
              "Selection and breeding": "Select useful compression travel and controlled return at the expected load. Return timing and damping are evaluated separately from maximal spring force.",
              "Builder contribution": "Fit joint travel, neutral posture and available leg clearance. Too little travel reaches a hard stop; poorly controlled return can lift the animal again.",
